Subject: Report a data member's C++ name and access specifier

`denote_data_member` says what a member is, but not what a generator has to
know before it may write code which names it.

The name it reports is the field's, put through `rust_mangle`: a member C++
calls `type` is reported as `type_`, which is the field bindgen generates and
is not the member. Anything emitting C++ against the member - an accessor, an
initializer - has to spell it as C++ does, and nothing else in the callback
surface says what that was.

The access specifier is not reported at all. It is recoverable from the
generated field only where `--respect-cxx-access-specs` is on, which is off by
default and which a `field_visibility` callback or a `private` annotation may
override in either direction - so reading it back off the field answers a
question about the Rust rendering, not about C++. A member which is private
is one a consumer must not emit an accessor for.

Both are already on `FieldMethods` for every member, bitfields included, so
this reports them beside the facts that callback already carries. Purely
additive: no generated code changes.

--- a/callbacks.rs
+++ b/callbacks.rs
@@ -584,6 +584,20 @@
     /// from this one but may carry a suffix to avoid colliding with a method.
     /// `None` for an unnamed bitfield, which gets neither.
     pub name: Option<&'a str>,
+
+    /// The member's name as C++ spells it, before the mangling `name` carries.
+    /// The two differ where the C++ name is not a Rust identifier - a member
+    /// called `type` becomes a field called `type_` - so this is the only one
+    /// of the pair which can be written back into C++ source. `None` for an
+    /// unnamed bitfield, as `name` is.
+    pub cpp_name: Option<&'a str>,
+
+    /// Whether C++ declared the member `public`. A generator which emits code
+    /// naming the member needs to know, and the generated Rust does not always
+    /// say: field visibility is what `--respect-cxx-access-specs` decides,
+    /// which is off by default and which a `field_visibility` callback or an
+    /// annotation may override either way.
+    pub is_public: bool,
 
     /// Whether C++ qualified the member's own type `const`, as opposed to it
     /// pointing at something `const`. Rust has no spelling for the former, so
--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -6550,6 +6550,8 @@
                     parent,
                     DataMemberInfo {
                         name: name.as_deref(),
+                        cpp_name: member.name(),
+                        is_public: member.is_public(),
                         is_const,
                         bitfield_width: member.bitfield_width(),
                         has_default_member_initializer: member
